WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.185
An AI assistant can appear to remember you because the application sends useful information along with your next message.

00:00:09.185 --> 00:00:13.094
A turn means one contribution to the conversation.

00:00:13.094 --> 00:00:18.782
Short-term memory keeps relevant recent turns available during the same chat.

00:00:18.782 --> 00:00:25.578
Long-term memory means deliberately saving information for use in a later conversation.

00:00:25.578 --> 00:00:33.398
Think of the difference between a note on your desk during a call and a preference a customer agrees to keep on file.

00:00:33.398 --> 00:00:40.140
A stored fact helps only if the application retrieves it for the right customer at the right time.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:45.804
Decide why you need the detail and how long it should last before saving it.

00:00:45.804 --> 00:00:50.117
Ravi gives Scout an order number and asks about his parcel.

00:00:50.117 --> 00:00:54.832
A moment later he asks whether the foam mat is in the same box.

00:00:54.832 --> 00:00:57.324
Read just that second question.

00:00:57.324 --> 00:01:01.414
You can understand the words, but which parcel does he mean?

00:01:01.414 --> 00:01:06.814
Without the previous turn, Scout asks for the order number all over again.

00:01:06.814 --> 00:01:10.454
Let's follow one turn and see where memory fits.

00:01:10.454 --> 00:01:12.753
A customer sends a message.

00:01:12.753 --> 00:01:20.048
The shop loads only information needed for this conversation that the customer is allowed to access.

00:01:20.048 --> 00:01:26.710
It builds the request: the system prompt, that memory, and the short-term messages so far.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:31.149
The model reads everything in the request and writes a reply.

00:01:31.149 --> 00:01:35.526
Then your code saves the new turn, and any fact worth keeping.

00:01:35.526 --> 00:01:41.114
Remember, the model only ever knows what is inside this one request.

00:01:41.114 --> 00:01:45.086
Inspect the information sent with the second request.

00:01:45.086 --> 00:01:52.136
Add the recent message that contains Ravi’s number, and same parcel now has a clear reference.

00:01:52.136 --> 00:01:55.025
Scout can look up the items in that order.

00:01:55.025 --> 00:02:02.313
Supplying his order number identifies the order; the contents still need to come from the order record.

00:02:02.313 --> 00:02:05.878
Read Ravi's follow-up without the earlier message.

00:02:05.878 --> 00:02:08.528
Is the mat in the same parcel?

00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:10.976
The word same points backwards.

00:02:10.976 --> 00:02:17.411
A new model request needs enough of that earlier exchange for the reference to mean something.

00:02:17.411 --> 00:02:20.986
Restore the order number and look up its contents.

00:02:20.986 --> 00:02:25.715
You can now answer his question, but only after checking the record.

00:02:25.715 --> 00:02:28.941
Memory helped identify the right parcel.

00:02:28.941 --> 00:02:32.976
It did not magically reveal which items were inside.

00:02:32.976 --> 00:02:38.832
A preference Ravi explicitly asks the shop to remember serves a different purpose.

00:02:38.832 --> 00:02:41.100
It might help on a later visit.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:50.036
Keeping an entire parcel conversation forever just because one preference is useful would store far more than the task needs.

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:54.562
Should the shop keep the entire chat forever to solve this problem?

00:02:54.562 --> 00:02:55.758
No.

00:02:55.758 --> 00:02:59.484
The earlier turn was needed for this conversation.

00:02:59.484 --> 00:03:05.555
A preferred size, if Ravi wants it saved for another visit, serves a different purpose.

00:03:05.555 --> 00:03:11.755
Ask which fact is needed next time, who may use it, and when it can be discarded.

00:03:11.755 --> 00:03:15.674
Not everything a customer says is worth remembering.

00:03:15.674 --> 00:03:22.952
The order number in this chat? Yes. It lives in the messages, and Scout needs it until the chat ends.

00:03:22.952 --> 00:03:29.901
A preferred colour or size? Worth saving to her profile, if it makes the next visit easier.

00:03:29.901 --> 00:03:37.819
Past orders already live in Orbit's order system, so Scout loads them from there instead of keeping its own copy.

00:03:37.819 --> 00:03:45.628
Card numbers, passwords and security codes? Never store them, anywhere. Mask them before anything is saved.

00:03:45.628 --> 00:03:51.926
And don't collect personal details just in case. If Scout doesn't need it, don't keep it.

00:03:51.926 --> 00:03:57.760
Every detail you store is something you must protect, and something that could leak.
